         Do not use this with ``--vo=gpu``. It will apply filtering twice, since
         most ``--vo=gpu`` options are unconditionally applied to the ``gpu``
         filter. There is no mechanism in mpv to prevent this.
+
+``motionblade=...``
+    Motion-compensated frame interpolation on the GPU with Vulkan. New frames
+    are computed between the source frames, so motion looks smoother on
+    displays with a higher refresh rate than the video.
+
+    The filter works on frames in system memory; hardware decoded frames are
+    copied there automatically. Supported formats are 8 to 16 bit
+    YUV with 4:2:0, 4:2:2 or 4:4:4 chroma, planar or semi-planar (``nv12``,
+    ``p010``). Other formats and frames smaller than 64x64 pass through
+    unchanged, as does everything when no suitable Vulkan device is found.
+
+    The filter holds one source frame back, so it can see where objects that
+    are hidden in one frame move next. Audio stays in sync.
+
+    Sub-options:
+
+    ``factor=<auto|2-8>``
+        A number multiplies the frame rate by that factor (default: 2).
+        ``auto`` outputs one frame per display refresh, using the display
+        refresh rate or ``--display-fps-override``. Videos with a frame rate at
+        or above the refresh rate pass through.
+
+    ``max-fps=<0-1000>``
+        Do not interpolate videos above this frame rate (default: 0, no limit).
+
+    ``max-gap=<0-10>``
+        Do not interpolate between frames that are more than this many seconds
+        apart (default: 0.25).
+
+    Example: ``--vf=motionblade --hwdec=auto``
